Now I see the issue! The `destFetchMonoLsb` function reads from the destination image data. If the destination image has uninitialized data (e.g., the image was created but not fully initialized), then the fetched values would be uninitialized.

But wait, the MSAN error is in `destStoreMonoLsb`, not `destFetchMonoLsb`. Let me look at `destStoreMonoLsb` again:

```cpp
static void QT_FASTCALL destStoreMonoLsb(QRasterBuffer *rasterBuffer, int x, int y, const uint *buffer, int length)
{
    uchar *Q_DECL_RESTRICT data = (uchar *)rasterBuffer->scanLine(y);
    if (rasterBuffer->monoDestinationWithClut) {
        for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
            if (buffer[i] == rasterBuffer->destColor0) {
                data[x >> 3] &= ~(1 << (x & 7));
            } else if (buffer[i] == rasterBuffer->destColor1) {
                data[x >> 3] |= 1 << (x & 7);
            } else if (findNearestColor(buffer[i], rasterBuffer) == rasterBuffer->destColor0) {
                data[x >> 3] &= ~(1 << (x & 7));
            } else {
                data[x >> 3] |= 1 << (x & 7);
            }
            ++x;
        }
    } else {
        for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
            if (qGray(buffer[i]) < int(qt_bayer_matrix[y & 15][x & 15]))
                data[x >> 3] |= 1 << (x & 7);
            else
                data[x >> 3] &= ~(1 << (x & 7));
            ++x;
        }
    }
}
```

The `buffer` parameter contains the blended pixel values. If the source image has uninitialized data, then `buffer[i]` would contain uninitialized values. The `qGray(buffer[i])` call would then read uninitialized data.

